The legal test for determining whether a respondent's request to withdraw admissions regarding the fair market values of certain shares should be granted is the "Want of Knowledge" test, as outlined in Rule 51.05 of the Ontario Rules of Civil Procedure, and also applicable in other Canadian jurisdictions.

The elements of this test are:
1. The party making the admission did not have sufficient knowledge of the facts to make an informed admission;
2. The admission was made in good faith and not for strategic or tactical reasons;
3. The party has a reasonable explanation for the want of knowledge at the time the admission was made;
4. The withdrawal of the admission will not cause prejudice to the other party.

The leading authority for this test is the Ontario Court of Appeal decision in McCann v. McLean (1983), 41 O.R. (2d) 1, which applied the test to determine whether a party should be allowed to withdraw an admission.

